{#
/**
 * @file
 * Case study rendered as a card on the work page.
 *
 * The card is the whole case study – there is no node page to click through
 * to, so nothing inside it links except the link out to the live site.
 *
 * Three parts:
 * - a scroll-snap strip of screenshots (no JS; one image collapses to a
 *   plain figure)
 * - the face: title, role, description
 * - the lower third: the value statement and the link out, separated by a
 *   rule. Always visible – it does not depend on hover.
 *
 * Available variables: see node.html.twig.
 */
#}
<article{{ attributes.addClass('bg-card rounded-4xl overflow-hidden flex flex-col w-full') }}>

  {{ title_prefix }}
  {{ title_suffix }}

  {# Loop the items rather than printing the field: Drupal wraps the whole
     field in one element, so styling `> *` would hit that wrapper and stack
     the images instead of laying them out in a row.

     The ratio sits on the wrapper, not the image – Tailwind's preflight sets
     `img { height: auto }`, which overrides `aspect-*` on the image itself.

     Which part of each screenshot survives the crop is decided by its focal
     point, not here: `3_2_wide` crops server-side, so the image arrives
     already at 3:2 and `object-position` would have nothing left to do. #}
  {% set shots = content.field_screenshots|filter((v, k) => k matches '/^\\d+$/') %}
  {% set scrolls = shots|length > 1 %}

  {% if shots is not empty %}
    {#
      Screenshots sit inset in the card with their own rounded frame, rather
      than running edge to edge: a screenshot is a picture of a screen, and it
      reads better as an object on the card than as the card's own surface.

      The strip carries no right-hand padding, so the next screenshot runs off
      the card edge and is clipped. That overhang is the only signal that the
      strip scrolls – quieter than dots or arrows, and it costs no markup.

      Geometry is the same on every card whatever the image count. Frames are
      a fixed fraction of the strip and width drives height on a 3:2 box, so
      anything that varied either the frame fraction or the strip's padding
      would make one card's screenshots taller than its neighbour's and knock
      the row's titles out of line. A lone screenshot is centred instead, so
      that it reads as inset rather than as a stalled scroll.

      `relative` clips absolutely positioned descendants to the scroller. The
      `3_2_card` media display hides its field label rather than rendering it
      visually-hidden, which is what used to escape this box and stretch the
      document sideways in Chrome; this keeps anything similar contained.

      Only a strip with something to scroll takes focus. Chrome makes scroll
      containers keyboard-focusable by itself, Safari does not, so the
      tabindex is what makes arrow-key scrolling work everywhere – and the
      group label stops it announcing as an unnamed region.
    #}
    <div class="relative flex snap-x snap-mandatory gap-4 overflow-x-auto p-4 pr-0 scroll-pl-4 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:-outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-rust md:p-6 md:pr-0 md:scroll-pl-6"
      {%- if scrolls %} tabindex="0" role="group" aria-label="{{ 'Screenshots of @title'|t({'@title': node.label}) }}"{% endif %}>
      {% for item in shots %}
        <div class="aspect-3/2 w-5/6 shrink-0 snap-start overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-ink/10 [&_img]:size-full [&_img]:object-cover {{ scrolls ? '' : 'mx-auto' }}">
          {{ item }}
        </div>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
  {% endif %}

  {# `grow` here rather than `mt-auto` on the lower third – same result,
     no top margin (spacing convention: margin-bottom and gap only). #}
  <div class="grow flex flex-col gap-3 p-4 md:p-6 border-b border-ink/10">
    {# h2: the page's h1 is 'Work' and each card is a top-level section under
       it, so there is no intermediate heading to sit beneath. #}
    {# Held at text-2xl rather than taking the responsive h2 scale (agreed
       2026-07-30). A 608px card is a compact unit; the page-level h2 size steps
       to 30px at md and makes a grid of six cards shout. Utilities beat the
       base layer, so this wins at every width. #}
    {% if label %}
      <h2 class="text-2xl">{{ label }}</h2>
    {% endif %}

    {# The role is meta, so it keeps Geist Mono while the title, description and
       value statement take the body face (agreed 2026-07-30).

       A `div`, not a `p`: the field renders its own `div`, and a div inside a p
       makes the parser close the paragraph early and hoist the div out of it, so
       nothing set on the wrapper reached the text. That had been quietly
       swallowing `text-base` and `text-ink-muted` too. #}
    {% if content.field_role|render|trim is not empty %}
      <div class="font-mono text-base text-ink-muted">{{ content.field_role }}</div>
    {% endif %}

    {# `text-lg` so the summary outranks the 16px mono role above it. At 16px
       the serif ran 7.7px a character against the mono's 9.6, which put the
       meta ahead of the content it labels. #}
    <div class="text-lg">{{ content.field_description }}</div>
  </div>

  {# Lower third: pinned to the bottom so cards of unequal height still line
     their value statements up across a row. #}
  {% if content.field_value|render|trim is not empty or content.field_link|render|trim is not empty %}
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-3 p-4 md:p-6">
      {# Serif, not mono (revised 2026-07-30). Mono here was tried and reversed:
         at the same px it is 24% wider per character, so the one-line claim
         outweighed the summary above it and the two fought instead of reading
         as statement-after-summary. The rule and the position already mark the
         change of register; the face does not need to as well. #}
      <div class="text-lg">{{ content.field_value }}</div>
      {# The link renders as a bare domain, which is a label rather than prose –
         Geist Mono, and monospace suits a URL.

         `[&_a]:font-mono` rather than `font-mono` on this wrapper: the base
         layer sets font-family directly on `a`, so an inherited family loses to
         it and the link came out in the body face. The descendant utility hits
         the anchor itself and lands in the utilities layer, which wins. #}
      {% if content.field_link|render|trim is not empty %}
        <div class="text-base [&_a]:font-mono [&_a]:text-rust [&_a]:underline [&_a]:underline-offset-4">
          {{ content.field_link }}
        </div>
      {% endif %}
    </div>
  {% endif %}

</article>
